---
/**
 * The 404, on the public shell. Plan 05-13, task 2.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * WHY THIS ROUTE EXISTS AT ALL
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * `wrangler.jsonc` sets `assets.not_found_handling` to `"404-page"`, which needs a
 * `dist/client/404.html` to serve — with no such file the Workers asset handler falls back to a
 * bare platform response with no navigation, no theme and no copy. MEASURED against real workerd
 * with this page in place: a request for `/nope-<random>` answers **404** and returns THIS
 * document, not a platform page.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * WHY IT CARRIES THE SHELL AND NOT A HAND-ROLLED DOCUMENT
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * The Phase 2 placeholder this replaces was a complete standalone `<html>` — its own `<head>`, its
 * own `<style>`, no nav, no footer, no theme script. A reader who mistypes a URL therefore left the
 * site's visual identity at the moment they most need a way back into it, and the page was
 * unthemed: a dark-mode reader got a white flash and a white page.
 *
 * On `PublicLayout` it inherits all of that for free — the nav, the footer, the single inline theme
 * block (so no flash), and the gutter ladder — which is also why the ladder gate keeps covering it.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * 🔴 IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT USE `<Seo>`, AND THAT IS THE OPPOSITE OF AN OVERSIGHT
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * Every other route in this phase renders `<Seo>` and SEO-01 requires the full tag set on every
 * page. This one must not, and the reason is specific rather than stylistic:
 *
 *   - `<Seo>` emits `<link rel="canonical">`. A canonical on a 404 declares the not-found URL to be
 *     a canonical page of this site, which is the definition of a soft 404. The status code says
 *     "this is not a page" and the canonical would say "this is the page" — a crawler resolves that
 *     contradiction in favour of the tag it can act on.
 *   - `<Seo>` emits `og:url`, `og:image` and `og:title`. A 404 sharing as a photograph of Singapore
 *     with a real-looking title is worse than sharing as nothing.
 *
 * So the head slot carries a `<title>` and the `noindex` this page has always had, and nothing
 * else. `test/public/seo.node.test.ts` excludes exactly `404.html` from the pages it audits, and
 * asserts that it is BOTH excluded from the sitemap AND present on disk — an exclusion asserted
 * against a document that does not exist is vacuously true, which is how this would have rotted.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * COPY — §13.2, VERBATIM, INCLUDING ONE JUDGEMENT ALREADY RECORDED
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 *     **Not found.**
 *     *There is nothing at this address.*
 *     → `Go to the home page`
 *
 * THE ARROW IS THE TABLE'S MARKER FOR "THEN A LINK", NOT COPY — it sits outside the backticks,
 * exactly as it does in the empty-category row directly above, where 05-07 recorded the same
 * reading in `PhotoEmpty.tsx`. Rows whose arrow IS copy carry it inside the backticks
 * (`ALL WORK →`, `SCROLL FOR THE WORK ↓`). The trailing period on "Not found." is likewise the
 * table's, and is kept for the same reason `PhotoEmpty` keeps "…yet.".
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * ZERO FRAMEWORK JAVASCRIPT (§5.1)
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * `Heading`, `Text` and `Link` are design-system React components rendered to static markup at
 * build time — no `client:*` directive, so no island, no hydration and no module script. They are
 * imported by subpath, never through the barrel, which `gate:ds` enforces.
 */

import { Heading } from '@akhil-saxena/design-system/components/Heading';
import { Text } from '@akhil-saxena/design-system/components/Text';
import PublicLayout from '../layouts/PublicLayout.astro';

/** Where the one link goes. Named so the markup and this file's own docstring cannot disagree. */
const WORK_PATH = '/development';
const PHOTOS_PATH = '/photography';
---

<PublicLayout mainClass="pub-max-home">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    {
      /*
       * NO EM DASH. Akhil: *"don't use em dashes in text"*, and the title was
       * `Not found — Akhil Saxena` — the one place a visual check would miss, because a browser tab
       * truncates it and no screenshot of the page shows it.
       */
    }
    <title>Not found · Akhil Saxena</title>
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
  </Fragment>

  {
    /*
     * ================================================================================================
     * P2, "THE PHOTOGRAPH THAT ISN'T" — the empty frame IS the illustration
     * ================================================================================================
     *
     * Akhil: *"Use p2 to build 404 page ... dont add home button"*, from the three 404 designs. P2 is
     * the two-column one: the message on the left, an empty picture frame on the right, captioned
     * "No image at this address".
     *
     * IT EARNS ITS PLACE ON THIS SITE SPECIFICALLY. A generic 404 illustration is decoration; an
     * empty frame on a PHOTOGRAPHER'S site is the same joke the page is already making, drawn once
     * instead of said twice. It is built from a border and a dashed inset rather than from an image,
     * so the page that says "no image here" does not itself load one.
     *
     * NO HOME BUTTON. The design's own third action was a quiet `home` link and it is dropped on
     * instruction. What remains is the two destinations that actually hold something — and the
     * wordmark in the bar is already a link home on every route, so nothing is lost.
     *
     * `aria-hidden` ON THE FRAME. Its caption repeats what the prose above already says, and a
     * screen reader meeting both hears the same fact twice. The frame is a picture of an absence;
     * the absence is stated in text.
     */
  }
  <div class="nf-page">
    <div class="nf-copy">
      <p class="nf-eyebrow">404 · Not found</p>
      {
        /* §4.3 item 1: the accent is the full stop and nothing else. `--ochre-d`, the same token
          `.wk-stop` uses — the design's green period is not taken, because the last chromatic accent
          left this palette on 2026-09-05 when the timeline's green dot went. */
      }
      <Heading level={1} size="4xl" weight="regular" className="nf-title">
        Nothing to see here<span class="nf-stop">.</span>
      </Heading>
      <Text variant="small" className="nf-sub">
        This address holds no page. An empty frame, no photograph in it.
      </Text>
      {
        /*
         * ==========================================================================================
         * NO ACTIONS AT ALL — THE BAR ALREADY CARRIES BOTH DESTINATIONS
         * ==========================================================================================
         *
         * Akhil: *"do i need buttons there? on 404 page. we have header already. it's duplicacy."*
         *
         * He is right, and it is the SAME RULE that removed the home button one step earlier —
         * *"dont add home button"* — applied consistently instead of to one link out of three. The
         * shell's nav ships `development` and `photography` on every route, this page included, and
         * the wordmark is the link home. Two buttons under the prose repeat all of it.
         *
         * P2's design has three actions. They earn their place in a static mock, where nothing else
         * on the canvas is clickable; on the real page they are the third copy of a menu the reader
         * can already see. What the page needs is to SAY that the address is empty, and it does.
         *
         * `.nf-acts` and `.nf-act` are gone from the stylesheet with them; `Button` is no longer
         * imported. `seo.node.test.ts` asserts the absence, because a 404 with no visible way out
         * reads like an oversight to the next person and this is the note saying it is not.
         */
      }
    </div>

    <div class="nf-frame" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="nf-frame-label">No image at this address</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</PublicLayout>

<style>
  /*
   * Layout only. Type, colour and radius come from the design system's tokens; nothing here
   * originates a value in a dimension it already names.
   */
  .nf-page {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-12);
    align-items: center;
    padding-block: var(--space-16);
  }

  /* Two columns only when the frame can be its own width without squeezing the prose. */
  @media (min-width: 673px) {
    .nf-page {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 340px;
      gap: var(--space-16);
    }
  }

  .nf-copy {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
  }

  .nf-eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-2xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-4);
  }

  .nf-stop {
    color: var(--ochre-d);
  }

  /*
   * 🔴 `padding`, NOT `margin`. `Heading` and `Text` both write `margin: 0` into the STYLE
   * ATTRIBUTE, which no stylesheet can reach at any specificity short of `!important`. The first
   * version used `margin-block-start` and the sub-paragraph rendered ON TOP of the heading's
   * descenders — measured, not guessed: "This address holds no page" overlapped "Nothing to see
   * here". `#ph-count` in `photography.css` carries the same note for the same reason.
   */
  /* padding, not margin: Heading and Text write margin:0 inline. */
  .nf-sub {
    /* A copy measure rather than a layout maximum, so it is in ch and not in the page ladder. */
    max-width: min(46ch, 100%);
    padding-block-start: var(--space-5);
  }

  .nf-title {
    padding-block-start: var(--space-1);
  }

  /*
   * THE FRAME IS DRAWN, NOT LOADED. A 1px edge with a dashed inset 12px inside it — the second
   * border is what makes it read as a mount rather than as an empty box, and both are `--wire` and
   * `--rule`, the two hairline weights this site already uses for bounding and separating.
   *
   * `aspect-ratio` rather than a fixed height, so the 340x250 of the design survives the column
   * narrowing at the breakpoint instead of leaving a fixed box in a fluid grid.
   */
  .nf-frame {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    aspect-ratio: 340 / 250;
    border: 1px solid var(--wire);
  }

  .nf-frame::before {
    position: absolute;
    inset: var(--space-3);
    border: 1px dashed var(--rule);
    content: '';
  }

  .nf-frame-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-2xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-4);
  }
</style>
